 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Change delivery via self-hosted WebSockets.** Connected browsers receive committed board changes over self-hosted WebSockets rather than five-second HTTPS polling or a hosted realtime service. Five-second polling was rejected because it trades push immediacy for repeated read traffic and multi-second lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring vendor fees and provider coupling, since the tenets permit self-hosted infrastructure and the 800-browser scale is manageable in-house. This commits the architecture to managing WebSocket connection capacity, reliable store-to-delivery publication, and browser reconnect/catch-up logic.
